 { A fortress is an ecosystem of mutual protection that the opponent cannot disrupt. Generally, after a fortress is created, only passive play is required to hold the draw. Sometimes, you can create fortresses using your knowledge of well-known theoretical endings. Other times, you may not be so fortunate and need to create an original fortress on your feet. To create an impregnable fortress, we need to ensure that the answer to the following three questions is a resounding ‘No’: 1. Does the attacker have any useful pawn breaks? 2. Can the attacker’s pieces, even when placed on their best plausible squares, disrupt the fortress? 3. Can the attacker force zugzwang? } 1. { -KEY- } Kh2 { In this position, there are clearly no pawns, so the first question is taken care of. To answer the second question, we must first identify the fortress. Here, White’s King defends the Bishop, which defends the Knight. At the same time, the Bishop and Knight create a force field around the White King, while the White King controls all the squares around the h1 corner. This means that neither the Black King nor the Black Queen can penetrate the fortress. To hold the draw, the White King simply needs to wait around in the safety of his fortress of three squares (h1, g1, and h2) . And all that’s left is the third question: Can Black force zugzwang? Let’s imagine a scenario where White’s King is on g1 and cannot legally move to h1 or h2. For that to happen, the Black Queen would have to be on the h-file. Wherever on the h-file she may be, White can always make a waiting move, Bh1. I chose Bh1 because it still leaves both of White’s pieces protected. After Bh1, the Bishop would lose control over f1 and h3. Our next task is to consider if there is any configuration of Black pieces where temporarily losing control over f1 or h3 is critical. There isn’t any such configuration. Hence, the fortress will hold. To put this into practice, I will present you with two positions. Your task is to determine the result (White wins, Black wins, or draw) . The solutions will be revealed in the positions after those. } 
 *

