 { What is the end position we are aiming for and how do we work towards it? } 1... { -KEY- } Nc6 { The plan here is to trap the White King in the corner and wait with Kc7-Kc8-Kc7-Kc8. After zugzwang eventually forces White to push his a2 pawn to a7, we will reroute our Knight to b6 or c7 to deliver checkmate. White's only hope is stalemate but his c-pawn will still have a couple of legal moves. Hence, White's goal is to push the c-pawn as far as it can (to c5) before pushing his a-pawn. Let’s consider a likely scenario a few moves later: It is Black to play, White’s pawns are on c5 and a7 while our King is on c8 and our Knight is still on c6. We can play Nc6-Nb4-Nd5-Nb6# in the time it takes for White to play c5-c6-c7. However, if Black’s King were on c7 instead of c8, the pawn would be stopped on the c6 square and White will get his stalemate. Can White achieve this position where our King is on c7 instead of c8? Yes. This is because he has the option to lose/gain tempi by starting with either c3 or c4. Black does not have this luxury. The King must strictly oscillate between c7 and c8. This means we need to start our Knight reroute a move earlier when White’s pawn is on a6. This position can be seen after White’s 6 move in the main variation. } 2. c4 Kc7 3. a4 Kc8 4. c5 Kc7 5. a5 Kc8 6. a6 { If our King moves to c7, White's pawn will be locked at a6 and he will escape thanks to stalemate. We must start our Knight reroute now. } Nd4 ( 6... Nb4 $4 7. Ka7 Nd5 8. c6 Kc7 9. Ka8 { Aside from heading for either c7 OR b6, the Black Knight must also control the a7 square to prevent Ka7 } ) 
7. Ka7 Kc7 { We must stop Kb6 } 8. c6 ( 8. Ka8 Nb5 9. c6 { This transposes to the main variation } Kc8 10. c7 Nxc7 11. Ka7 Nd5 12. Ka8 Kc7 13. Ka7 Ne7 14. Ka8 Nc8 15. a7 Nb6 ) 
Nb5+ 9. Ka8 Kc8 10. c7 Nxc7+ 11. Ka7 Nd5 { Again, we must stop Kb6 } 12. Ka8 { Now our next goal is to control a7 with our Knight while preparing for either Nb6# OR Nc7#. The two candidates are b5 and c8. At the same time, our King should be on c7 to prevent Kb6. That means Nd5-Nc3-Nb5-Nc7# will not be possible. Instead, we should go for Nd5-Ne7-Nc8-Nb6# } Kc7 ( 12... Nc3 $4 { Heading for c7 via b5 is not possible. We don't have enough time } 13. a7 ( 12... Kc7 12. Ka7 { Even if White played Ka7, we cannot win with the Nb5-Nc7# plan } Kc7 14. Ka8 Nb5 15. a7 { If only c7 were vacant } ) 
 ) 
13. Ka7 Ne7 14. Ka8 Nc8 15. a7 Nb6# 
