1. { -KEY- } Ra6 { Our goal is to play Be5#. We can threaten it by moving our d6 Rook while attacking the Black Rook with either Ra6 or Rd3. Ra6 forces ...Re3 while Rd3 forces ...Ra5. Which is worse for Black? } ( 1. Rd3 Ra5 { Black's Rook has many squares along the fifth rank to keep an eye on the critical e5 square. Black will escape with ...Kg7 next } ) 
Re3 { Forcing the Black Rook to the very short e-file. Our next goal is to force the Rook of the e-file. There are only three squares we need to take care of, e3, e4, and e5. Since our King is glued to the e1 square, it is up to the rest of our pieces. How can our Rook and Bishop control a total of three squares? From the a-file, our Rook can control one square at a time, so it is up to the Bishop watch over the remaining two squares. For our Bishop to control two squares on the e-file, it will have to be on f4, where it controls e3 and e5. The final square, e4, is for our Rook to control. Now we have the final position in mind - our Bishop should be on f4 while our Rook should be on a4. But let's not forget that Black is just one move (...Kg7) away from removing all mating ideas. This is why all our moves need to come with tempo } 2. Ra3 $1 { Forcing 2...Re4 so that we can we can play 3. Ra4 with tempo } ( 2. Bf4 $4 Re4 3. Ra4 Rxf4 4. Rxf4 ) 
Re4 $1 3. Ra4 Re3 4. Bf4 $1 { All the squares along the e-file are taken away. Black will either get mated or lose his Rook. } 
