 { Stalemate is not merely the result of one side playing the ending carelessly. As we will see in the following positions, stalemate is a key defensive resource that can be deployed even at the highest level when facing an imminent loss. Generally speaking, as the defender, there are two strategies to force stalemate. The first can be used when your King already has no (or very few) legal moves. All you need to do is craftily jettison your remaining pieces on the correct squares, in the correct order. The second strategy is to rush to trap your King with your own pieces. To set this up, you need to identify all the squares that the opponent controls on the board. Every one of them. Afterwards, it will be clear where all your pieces belong to create the stalemate refuge. For example, in this position Black is about to use his h-pawn to draw the enemy King to the kingside and then capture all the undefended queenside pawns with his own King. Just before thinking about resigning, White should try and find a stalemate refuge here. } 1. { -KEY- } Kc2 { Now that we have highlighted all the squares under Black’s control, it is clear that the White King should be headed for a4. Once the monarch reaches a4, we will complete our stalemate refuge with a3. And there is nothing Black can do to stop this plan. } h5 2. Kb2 h4 3. Ka3 h3 4. Ka4 h2 5. a3 { Now let’s practice stalemating ourselves with the following puzzles. } 
