 { Sometimes, a long-ranged piece may seem quite free on an open board. However, upon further analysis, you may realise that every potential square it can go is somehow not available due to a myriad of tactics (such as a skewer or a fork) . This is what I call the Illusion of Freedom. } 1. { -KEY- } Ka2 { For example, in this position, Black’s Bishop seems spoilt for choice with all the squares along the a1-h8 diagonal. However, as it turns out, no matter where the Bishop goes, the White Knight will eventually win it with a fork. Let’s explore the variations. } Bh8 ( 1... Be5 2. Nf7 ) 
 ( 1... Bc3 2. c7 Kxc7 3. Nb5 ) 
 ( 1... Bd4 2. c7 Kxc7 3. Nb5 ) 
 ( 1... Bf6 2. c7 Kxc7 3. Ne8 ) 
 ( 1... Bg7 2. c7 Kxc7 3. Ne8 ) 
2. Nf7+ { Similar to this example, many Illusion of Freedom puzzles involve Knight forks and double attacks. Therefore, knowing the Knight well is winning half the battle against this quirky set of puzzles. And that is exactly what this section focuses - to help you become a Knight virtuoso. A key part of understanding the Knight’s peculiar movement lies in recognising its interaction with colour complexes. On the most fundamental level, a Knight on a light square will control dark squares only. Conversely, a Knight on a dark square controls light squares only. Building on this observation, this means that two pieces are forkable by a Knight only if they are on the same colour complex. In this position, the Black King would either be on d8 or c7 (depending on whether we played c7+) , both of which are dark squares. This was the reason Black’s dark-squared Bishop had no escape from the Knight. The Knight was on d6 (a dark square) , ready to jump to the neighbouring light squares to fork the two Black pieces on dark squares. As you work your way through the next few puzzles, we will see this concept in action in scenarios where the Knight needs to make more than two moves and where the Superposition Technique can be used. Keeping the colour complexes in mind, let’s see the Illusion of Freedom in action. } 
