1. { -KEY- } c7 Qxc7 { Forced } 2. Nb5 { To efficiently confirm if the Black Queen is trapped, we will need to use the Superposition Technique. Wherever the Queen goes, we will have the option to play 3.Nd4+, forcing 3...Ke5. After that, we will have yet another option to play 4.Ng6+ which forces 4...Kd6. This means that the Black King is in a superposition of three squares - f5, e5, and d6. Whichever square allows a Knight fork, we will force the King there. Bearing this in mind and returning to the current position, if the Queen moves to a light square, we should leave the King on f5, a light square, and look for a Knight fork. If the Queen moves to a dark square, we should play 3.Nd4+, forcing the King to move to e5, a dark square. After 3...Ke5, we should look for a Knight fork. If the King and Queen still cannot be forked, all is not lost. We can play 4.Ng6+, forcing the Black King to d6, a different dark square, before looking for a Knight fork again. Using the Superposition Technique in this way, working through all the variations will be a breeze. } Qg7 ( 2... Qb6 3. Nd4 Ke5 4. Nd7 ) 
 ( 2... Qa5 3. Nd4 Ke5 4. Nc6 ) 
 ( 2... Qb7 3. Nd6 ) 
 ( 2... Qb8 3. Nd4 Ke5 4. Nc6 ) 
 ( 2... Qd8 3. Nd4 Ke5 4. Nc6 ) 
 ( 2... Qg3 3. Nd4 Ke5 4. Ng6 Kd6 5. Nf5 ) 
 ( 2... Qh2 3. Nd4 Ke5 4. Nf3 ) 
 ( 2... Qe7 3. Nd4 Ke5 4. Ng6 ) 
 ( 2... Qf7 3. Nd6 ) 
